"""Bounded sequential-decision and survival illustrations; no fitted empirical claims."""
import math
from math_ops import op,OPS

def add(n,lab,out,ts,py,q,e,guard=None,plot=None):
 op(n,lab,out,ts,py,q,e,'curve' if plot else 'bars')
 if guard:OPS[n]['guard']=guard
 if plot:OPS[n]['plot']=plot
add('season','Level Trend Amplitude Time','Level','[p[0]+p[1]*p[3]+p[2]*Math.sin(2*Math.PI*p[3])]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]*p[3]+p[2]*math.sin(2*math.pi*p[3])],'A toy signal is Level {Level}+Trend {Trend}×Time {Time}+Amplitude {Amplitude}×sin(2πTime). What signal results?','An additive decomposition separates the assumed components. This calculation does not estimate them from observations.',plot=[3,0,3])
add('volupdate','Return Variance Weight','Variance','[(1-p[2])*p[1]+p[2]*p[0]*p[0]]',lambda p:[(1-p[2])*p[1]+p[2]*p[0]**2],'Update toy variance {Variance} toward squared Return {Return}, giving it Weight {Weight}. What new variance results?','A large squared return increases this explicit volatility estimate. This recurrence is not a fitted stochastic-volatility posterior.','p[1]<0||p[2]<0||p[2]>1')
add('assignment','TrackA TrackB DetectA DetectB','CostGap Straight Swapped','[(p[0]-p[2])**2+(p[1]-p[3])**2-(p[0]-p[3])**2-(p[1]-p[2])**2,(p[0]-p[2])**2+(p[1]-p[3])**2,(p[0]-p[3])**2+(p[1]-p[2])**2]',lambda p:[(p[0]-p[2])**2+(p[1]-p[3])**2-(p[0]-p[3])**2-(p[1]-p[2])**2,(p[0]-p[2])**2+(p[1]-p[3])**2,(p[0]-p[3])**2+(p[1]-p[2])**2],'Two toy tracks are {TrackA},{TrackB}; detections {DetectA},{DetectB}. What is straight assignment squared error minus swapped assignment squared error?','The lower-cost association depends on both locations. This is one association step, not a multi-target tracker.')
add('gauge','Robot Landmark Shift','Range RobotShift LandmarkShift','[p[1]-p[0],p[0]+p[2],p[1]+p[2]]',lambda p:[p[1]-p[0],p[0]+p[2],p[1]+p[2]],'A toy robot is at {Robot}, landmark at {Landmark}. Shift both by {Shift}. What landmark-minus-robot displacement remains?','Relative displacement survives a shared translation. Absolute position requires an anchor; this is one SLAM identifiability ingredient.')
add('optimism','Mean Total Pulls','UCB Bonus','[p[0]+Math.sqrt(2*Math.log(p[1])/p[2]),Math.sqrt(2*Math.log(p[1])/p[2])]',lambda p:[p[0]+math.sqrt(2*math.log(p[1])/p[2]),math.sqrt(2*math.log(p[1])/p[2])],'A toy UCB score adds sqrt(2 log Total/Pulls) to Mean {Mean}. Total {Total}, Pulls {Pulls}: what score results?','Less sampled arms receive a larger optimism bonus. This one-step score does not establish cumulative regret.','p[1]<1||p[2]<=0')
add('betamom','Alpha Beta Wins Losses','Mean Variance','[(p[0]+p[2])/(p[0]+p[1]+p[2]+p[3]),(p[0]+p[2])*(p[1]+p[3])/(Math.pow(p[0]+p[1]+p[2]+p[3],2)*(p[0]+p[1]+p[2]+p[3]+1))]',lambda p:[(p[0]+p[2])/sum(p),(p[0]+p[2])*(p[1]+p[3])/(sum(p)**2*(sum(p)+1))],'A toy Beta({Alpha},{Beta}) prior sees {Wins} wins and {Losses} losses. What posterior mean results?','Posterior parameters add successes and failures separately. Sampling from this posterior is an additional Thompson-sampling step.','p[0]<=0||p[1]<=0||p[2]<0||p[3]<0')
add('regret','Best Chosen Pulls','Regret','[(p[0]-p[1])*p[2]]',lambda p:[(p[0]-p[1])*p[2]],'The best toy arm pays mean {Best}; a chosen arm pays {Chosen} for {Pulls} pulls. What expected cumulative regret results?','Regret compares expected rewards with an oracle benchmark. Realized rewards can fluctuate around this expected cost.')
add('contextpolicy','Share AA AB BA BB','Gain Personalized Global','[p[0]*Math.max(p[1],p[2])+(1-p[0])*Math.max(p[3],p[4])-Math.max(p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[3],p[0]*p[2]+(1-p[0])*p[4]),p[0]*Math.max(p[1],p[2])+(1-p[0])*Math.max(p[3],p[4]),Math.max(p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[3],p[0]*p[2]+(1-p[0])*p[4])]',lambda p:[p[0]*max(p[1:3])+(1-p[0])*max(p[3:5])-max(p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[3],p[0]*p[2]+(1-p[0])*p[4]),p[0]*max(p[1:3])+(1-p[0])*max(p[3:5]),max(p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[3],p[0]*p[2]+(1-p[0])*p[4])],'Type A share {Share} has rewards AA/AB={AA}/{AB}; type B has BA/BB={BA}/{BB}. What is the gain from best-by-type over best-global choice?','Known heterogeneous rewards can favor a personalized policy. Learning these rewards and evaluating a policy require additional data.','p[0]<0||p[0]>1')
add('streamvalue','Reward Gamma','Value','[p[0]/(1-p[1])]',lambda p:[p[0]/(1-p[1])],'A known toy arm pays Reward {Reward} forever with discount Gamma {Gamma}. What discounted value results?','This known-payoff benchmark has no exploration value. It is not a Gittins index for an uncertain arm.','p[1]<0||p[1]>=1')
add('bellman','Reward Gamma Next','Value','[p[0]+p[1]*p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]*p[2]],'A toy one-step backup uses Reward {Reward}+Gamma {Gamma}×Next {Next}. What backed-up value results?','Immediate reward and discounted continuation both matter. One backup does not solve a complete decision process.')
add('qupdate','Q Alpha Reward Gamma Next','Q Target','[p[0]+p[1]*(p[2]+p[3]*p[4]-p[0]),p[2]+p[3]*p[4]]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]*(p[2]+p[3]*p[4]-p[0]),p[2]+p[3]*p[4]],'Toy Q {Q} moves by Alpha {Alpha} toward Reward {Reward}+Gamma {Gamma}×Next {Next}. What updated Q results?','The sampled target changes a value estimate. Which continuation is supplied distinguishes different learning updates.','p[1]<0||p[1]>1')
add('policygradient','P Action Reward Baseline Step','NewP Gradient','[1/(1+Math.exp(-(Math.log(p[0]/(1-p[0]))+p[4]*(p[2]-p[3])*(p[1]-p[0])))),(p[2]-p[3])*(p[1]-p[0])]',lambda p:[1/(1+math.exp(-(math.log(p[0]/(1-p[0]))+p[4]*(p[2]-p[3])*(p[1]-p[0])))),(p[2]-p[3])*(p[1]-p[0])],'For a toy Bernoulli policy P {P}, observed Action {Action}, Reward {Reward}, Baseline {Baseline}, Step {Step}, update its logit by Step×(Reward-Baseline)×(Action-P). What new P results?','A score-function step changes the action probability using centered reward. One stochastic step does not guarantee improvement.','p[0]<=0||p[0]>=1||![0,1].includes(p[1])')
add('shaped','Reward Gamma PhiNow PhiNext','Reward','[p[0]+p[1]*p[3]-p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]*p[3]-p[2]],'Add potential shaping Gamma {Gamma}×PhiNext {PhiNext}-PhiNow {PhiNow} to Reward {Reward}. What shaped reward results?','Potential differences provide intermediate feedback. Policy-invariance claims additionally require compatible discounting, boundaries and a full MDP.')
add('stopvalue','Offer Continuation Gamma','Value','[Math.max(p[0],p[1]*p[2])]',lambda p:[max(p[0],p[1]*p[2])],'A toy seller can take Offer {Offer} or discounted continuation {Continuation}×Gamma {Gamma}. What is the better expected value?','Stopping compares the offer with a supplied continuation value. Computing that continuation is a separate dynamic-programming problem.')
add('maxnoise','Arms Scale','Bias','[p[1]*(1-Math.pow(2,1-p[0]))]',lambda p:[p[1]*(1-2**(1-p[0]))],'Each of {Arms} equal-value arms has independent noise ±Scale {Scale}, each equally likely. What is the expected maximum estimation error?','Taking a maximum selects positive noise unless every estimate is low. Independent evaluation avoids this selection in the stated symmetric model.','p[0]<1||!Number.isInteger(p[0])||p[1]<0')
add('km','N1 D1 N2 D2','Survival','[(1-p[1]/p[0])*(1-p[3]/p[2])]',lambda p:[(1-p[1]/p[0])*(1-p[3]/p[2])],'Two toy event times have risk sets N1 {N1}, N2 {N2} and failures D1 {D1}, D2 {D2}. What Kaplan-Meier survival product results?','Each drop uses the risk set just before that event time. Censoring assumptions matter for interpreting the estimated curve.','p[0]<=0||p[2]<=0||p[1]<0||p[1]>p[0]||p[3]<0||p[3]>p[2]')
add('piecehazard','Rate1 Time1 Rate2 Time2','Survival','[Math.exp(-p[0]*p[1]-p[2]*p[3])]',lambda p:[math.exp(-p[0]*p[1]-p[2]*p[3])],'A toy lifetime has hazard Rate1 {Rate1} for Time1 {Time1}, then Rate2 {Rate2} for Time2 {Time2}. What survival probability results?','Survival is the exponential of negative cumulative hazard. These stipulated rates need not describe a real population.','p.some(v=>v<0)')
add('weibull','Shape Scale Time','Survival Hazard','[Math.exp(-Math.pow(p[2]/p[1],p[0])),p[0]/p[1]*Math.pow(p[2]/p[1],p[0]-1)]',lambda p:[math.exp(-(p[2]/p[1])**p[0]),p[0]/p[1]*(p[2]/p[1])**(p[0]-1)],'A toy Weibull lifetime has Shape {Shape}, Scale {Scale}. What survival probability remains at Time {Time}?','The shape parameter changes how hazard varies with age. The model is illustrative rather than a fitted reliability forecast.','p.some(v=>v<=0)',[2,.05,12])
add('ph','S0 HR','Survival','[Math.pow(p[0],p[1])]',lambda p:[p[0]**p[1]],'Baseline survival is S0 {S0}. A stipulated proportional hazard ratio is HR {HR}. What comparison survival S0^HR results?','A hazard ratio acts on cumulative hazard rather than subtracting a fixed survival probability. Proportionality is an assumption.','p[0]<0||p[0]>1||p[1]<=0')
add('competing','Rate1 Rate2 Time','Incidence Naive','[p[0]/(p[0]+p[1])*(1-Math.exp(-(p[0]+p[1])*p[2])),1-Math.exp(-p[0]*p[2])]',lambda p:[p[0]/(p[0]+p[1])*(1-math.exp(-(p[0]+p[1])*p[2])),1-math.exp(-p[0]*p[2])],'Two constant toy cause-specific hazards are {Rate1},{Rate2}. By Time {Time}, what probability has failed from cause 1?','A competing event removes a person from future risk. Ignoring that event gives a different estimand.','p[0]<=0||p[1]<0||p[2]<0',[2,0,10])
add('cure','Fraction Rate Time','Survival','[p[0]+(1-p[0])*Math.exp(-p[1]*p[2])]',lambda p:[p[0]+(1-p[0])*math.exp(-p[1]*p[2])],'Toy cure Fraction {Fraction} never fails; others have exponential Rate {Rate}. What survival remains at Time {Time}?','A mixture with a nonfailing component has a positive survival plateau. A finite observation window cannot by itself prove a cure fraction.','p[0]<0||p[0]>1||p[1]<0||p[2]<0',[2,0,12])
add('rmst','Rate Horizon','RMST','[p[0]===0?p[1]:(1-Math.exp(-p[0]*p[1]))/p[0]]',lambda p:[p[1] if p[0]==0 else (1-math.exp(-p[0]*p[1]))/p[0]],'A toy exponential lifetime has Rate {Rate}. What is expected survival time restricted to Horizon {Horizon}?','Restricted mean survival integrates survival only up to the chosen horizon. It has units of time and depends on that horizon.','p[0]<0||p[1]<0',[1,0,10])
add('aft','Rate Stretch Time','Survival','[Math.exp(-p[0]*p[2]/p[1])]',lambda p:[math.exp(-p[0]*p[2]/p[1])],'A toy exponential Rate {Rate} has its lifetime multiplied by Stretch {Stretch}. What survival remains at Time {Time}?','Stretching time changes when a survival probability is reached. This one exponential model does not establish a general hazard-ratio interpretation.','p[0]<0||p[1]<=0||p[2]<0',[2,0,10])
add('frailty','Share Fast Slow Time','Hazard Survival','[(p[0]*p[1]*Math.exp(-p[1]*p[3])+(1-p[0])*p[2]*Math.exp(-p[2]*p[3]))/(p[0]*Math.exp(-p[1]*p[3])+(1-p[0])*Math.exp(-p[2]*p[3])),p[0]*Math.exp(-p[1]*p[3])+(1-p[0])*Math.exp(-p[2]*p[3])]',lambda p:[(p[0]*p[1]*math.exp(-p[1]*p[3])+(1-p[0])*p[2]*math.exp(-p[2]*p[3]))/(p[0]*math.exp(-p[1]*p[3])+(1-p[0])*math.exp(-p[2]*p[3])),p[0]*math.exp(-p[1]*p[3])+(1-p[0])*math.exp(-p[2]*p[3])],'A toy population starts with Share {Share} at constant hazard Fast {Fast}; others at Slow {Slow}. What population hazard remains at Time {Time}?','Higher-risk people leave the surviving population sooner. Its mixture hazard can fall while each individual hazard stays constant.','p[0]<0||p[0]>1||p[1]<0||p[2]<0||p[3]<0',[3,0,8])
add('maxuniform','X N','CDF Mean','[Math.pow(p[0],p[1]),p[1]/(p[1]+1)]',lambda p:[p[0]**p[1],p[1]/(p[1]+1)],'For {N} independent Uniform(0,1) draws, what is probability their maximum is at most X {X}?','All draws must fall below the threshold. The bounded maximum approaches its endpoint rather than growing without limit.','p[0]<0||p[0]>1||p[1]<1',[0,0,1])
add('gumbel','X Location Scale','CDF','[Math.exp(-Math.exp(-(p[0]-p[1])/p[2]))]',lambda p:[math.exp(-math.exp(-(p[0]-p[1])/p[2]))],'A stipulated Gumbel distribution has Location {Location}, Scale {Scale}. What is its CDF at X {X}?','This is one extreme-value family. Selecting a family for data requires tail assumptions and validation.','p[2]<=0',[0,-3,6])
add('returnlevel','Period Rate','Level','[Math.log(p[0])/p[1]]',lambda p:[math.log(p[0])/p[1]],'Independent annual toy levels are exponential with Rate {Rate}. What threshold has annual exceedance probability 1/Period {Period}?','A return period describes a yearly exceedance probability. It does not schedule one event every fixed number of years.','p[0]<=1||p[1]<=0')
add('paretosurvival','Minimum Shape Threshold','Tail','[Math.pow(p[0]/p[2],p[1])]',lambda p:[(p[0]/p[2])**p[1]],'A toy Pareto distribution has Minimum {Minimum}, Shape {Shape}. What probability exceeds Threshold {Threshold}?','A tail exponent controls how rapidly large-event probability falls. The stipulated distribution is not evidence that a real dataset is Pareto.','p[0]<=0||p[1]<=0||p[2]<p[0]',[2,1,20])
add('shock','Shock IndependentP','Conditional Marginal Joint','[(p[0]+(1-p[0])*p[1]*p[1])/(p[0]+(1-p[0])*p[1]),p[0]+(1-p[0])*p[1],p[0]+(1-p[0])*p[1]*p[1]]',lambda p:[(p[0]+(1-p[0])*p[1]**2)/(p[0]+(1-p[0])*p[1]),p[0]+(1-p[0])*p[1],p[0]+(1-p[0])*p[1]**2],'A toy common crash occurs with probability Shock {Shock}; otherwise two crashes occur independently with IndependentP {IndependentP}. Given one crash, what probability is the other?','A common shock changes joint tail risk. This explicit mixture is not a Gaussian copula fit.','p[0]<0||p[0]>1||p[1]<=0||p[1]>1')
add('layer','Loss Attachment Limit','Payout','[Math.min(Math.max(p[0]-p[1],0),p[2])]',lambda p:[min(max(p[0]-p[1],0),p[2])],'A toy loss is {Loss}. A layer attaches at {Attachment} and pays at most Limit {Limit}. What payout results?','Layering separates which part of a loss is covered. Pricing requires the loss distribution and other contract assumptions.','p.some(v=>v<0)',[0,0,200])
add('recordchance','N','Probability','[1/p[0]]',lambda p:[1/p[0]],'For N {N} independent identically distributed continuous observations, what chance is the last a new upper record?','Exchangeability makes every observation equally likely to be the largest. Trend and ties can break this argument.','p[0]<1')
add('messages','Prior LR1 LR2','Posterior','[(p[0]*p[1]*p[2])/(1-p[0]+p[0]*p[1]*p[2])]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]*p[2]/(1-p[0]+p[0]*p[1]*p[2])],'Toy prior {Prior} receives two conditionally independent evidence likelihood ratios LR1 {LR1}, LR2 {LR2}. What posterior results?','Likelihood messages multiply odds under the stated conditional independence. Dependent evidence must not be counted as independent.','p[0]<=0||p[0]>=1||p[1]<=0||p[2]<=0')
add('partialcorr','DiagA Off DiagB','Correlation','[-p[1]/Math.sqrt(p[0]*p[2])]',lambda p:[-p[1]/math.sqrt(p[0]*p[2])],'A toy Gaussian precision block has diagonals {DiagA},{DiagB}, off-diagonal {Off}. What partial correlation is implied?','A zero precision entry encodes conditional independence in a nonsingular Gaussian model. Correlation is not a causal direction.','p[0]<=0||p[2]<=0||p[1]*p[1]>=p[0]*p[2]')
add('commonfactor','Common NoiseA NoiseB','Correlation','[p[0]/Math.sqrt((p[0]+p[1])*(p[0]+p[2]))]',lambda p:[p[0]/math.sqrt((p[0]+p[1])*(p[0]+p[2]))],'Two toy measurements share variance Common {Common} and have independent noise variances {NoiseA},{NoiseB}. What correlation results?','A shared latent component induces association without a direct edge between measurements. This covariance construction does not identify the latent cause from data.','p.some(v=>v<0)||p[0]+p[1]<=0||p[0]+p[2]<=0')
add('forwardnoise','Data Noise Alpha','Noisy','[Math.sqrt(p[2])*p[0]+Math.sqrt(1-p[2])*p[1]]',lambda p:[math.sqrt(p[2])*p[0]+math.sqrt(1-p[2])*p[1]],'For toy Data {Data}, realized standard-normal Noise {Noise}, and Alpha {Alpha}, what is sqrt(Alpha)Data+sqrt(1-Alpha)Noise?','This forward noising coordinate reduces signal weight as alpha falls. It is not a trained image diffusion model.','p[2]<0||p[2]>1',[2,0,1])
add('denoise','Noisy Alpha','Mean Variance','[Math.sqrt(p[1])*p[0],1-p[1]]',lambda p:[math.sqrt(p[1])*p[0],1-p[1]],'With a standard-normal prior and toy noised observation {Noisy}=sqrt(Alpha {Alpha})X+sqrt(1-Alpha)ε, what posterior mean of X results?','Gaussian conditioning gives a denoised mean and remaining variance. A posterior mean is not a generated image or a reverse-sampling trajectory.','p[1]<0||p[1]>1')
add('score','X Mean Variance','Score','[-(p[0]-p[1])/p[2]]',lambda p:[-(p[0]-p[1])/p[2]],'For a toy Normal(Mean {Mean}, Variance {Variance}), what is the derivative of log density at X {X}?','The score points toward increasing log density. Density-gradient drift alone does not draw correctly distributed samples.','p[2]<=0',[0,-4,4])
add('uncertainty','Noise ParameterVar N','Total Noise Parameter','[p[0]+p[1]/p[2],p[0],p[1]/p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]/p[2],p[0],p[1]/p[2]],'Toy prediction variance is irreducible Noise {Noise}+ParameterVar {ParameterVar}/N {N}. What total variance results?','Only the specified parameter component shrinks with more data. The decomposition assumes this particular model.','p[0]<0||p[1]<0||p[2]<=0')
add('watermark','Count N NullP','Z','[(p[0]-p[1]*p[2])/Math.sqrt(p[1]*p[2]*(1-p[2]))]',lambda p:[(p[0]-p[1]*p[2])/math.sqrt(p[1]*p[2]*(1-p[2]))],'A toy token tally has Count {Count} green tokens among N {N}, with independent null chance NullP {NullP}. What standardized count results?','This score uses an independent Bernoulli null variance. Dependence, editing and key selection affect any actual watermark test.','p[1]<=0||p[0]<0||p[0]>p[1]||p[2]<=0||p[2]>=1')
add('brier','Y P','Loss','[(p[0]-p[1])**2]',lambda p:[(p[0]-p[1])**2],'Binary toy outcome Y {Y} was forecast with P {P}. What squared probability error results?','A Brier score evaluates a probability against an outcome. Repeated forecasts are needed to assess calibration.','![0,1].includes(p[0])||p[1]<0||p[1]>1')
add('expectedbrier','Belief Report','Loss Regret','[p[0]*(1-p[1])**2+(1-p[0])*p[1]*p[1],(p[1]-p[0])**2]',lambda p:[p[0]*(1-p[1])**2+(1-p[0])*p[1]**2,(p[1]-p[0])**2],'Your toy belief is {Belief}; you report {Report}. What expected Brier loss follows under that belief?','Expected squared probability error is minimized by reporting the belief itself. The belief need not equal the true event rate.','p.some(v=>v<0||v>1)',[1,0,1])
add('predwidth','SD N Z Prediction','HalfWidth','[p[2]*p[0]*Math.sqrt((p[3]===1?1:0)+1/p[1])]',lambda p:[p[2]*p[0]*math.sqrt((1 if p[3]==1 else 0)+1/p[1])],'Known toy SD {SD}, N {N}, Z multiplier {Z}. Prediction {Prediction}=1 targets a new observation, 0 the mean. What normal interval half-width results?','An individual prediction includes irreducible observation noise. These normal intervals assume a known variance and independent data.','p[0]<=0||p[1]<=0||p[2]<=0||![0,1].includes(p[3])')
add('extremize','P Strength','Probability','[1/(1+Math.exp(-p[1]*Math.log(p[0]/(1-p[0]))))]',lambda p:[1/(1+math.exp(-p[1]*math.log(p[0]/(1-p[0]))))],'Multiply toy log odds of P {P} by Strength {Strength}. What transformed probability results?','Strength above one pushes probabilities toward extremes. Whether this improves accuracy requires held-out forecast evidence.','p[0]<=0||p[0]>=1||p[1]<0',[0,.01,.99])
add('confidencewidth','N Alpha Safe','HalfWidth','[Math.sqrt(Math.log(p[2]===1?2*p[0]*(p[0]+1)/p[1]:2/p[1])/(2*p[0]))]',lambda p:[math.sqrt(math.log(2*p[0]*(p[0]+1)/p[1] if p[2]==1 else 2/p[1])/(2*p[0]))],'For independent [0,1] toy data use N {N}, error Alpha {Alpha}. Safe {Safe}=1 allocates α/[n(n+1)] per look; 0 uses α at one fixed look. What Hoeffding half-width results?','The summable allocation permits a union bound over all positive integer looks. It is conservative and assumes independent bounded observations.','p[0]<1||p[1]<=0||p[1]>=1||![0,1].includes(p[2])')
